WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:06.13
This is journey seventeen of Hewn, the software factory: trace a requirement.

00:00:06.13 --> 00:00:11.07
Every requirement Hewn writes should end up designed, walked through in a journey,

00:00:11.07 --> 00:00:12.15
tested and merged.

00:00:12.15 --> 00:00:15.94
Owners and Viewers want to pick any L2 and see where it stands,

00:00:15.94 --> 00:00:18.95
and to spot the ones that have no acceptance test.

00:00:18.95 --> 00:00:23.65
This video walks through how it was built: reading the repository at its head,

00:00:23.65 --> 00:00:28.70
the rules that decide what names a requirement, the Traceability tab, and the tests.

00:00:29.85 --> 00:00:35.47
A user opens an L2 in Traceability and sees its L1, the designs that cover it,

00:00:35.47 --> 00:00:40.88
the journeys it appears in, the test files whose traces to header names it,

00:00:40.88 --> 00:00:43.98
and the pull requests whose titles name it.

00:00:43.98 --> 00:00:49.09
An L2 with no test is marked no acceptance test, unless it is deferred.

00:00:50.24 --> 00:00:51.45
Here is the flow.

00:00:51.45 --> 00:00:56.23
The design planned a background indexer and a table of trace links.

00:00:56.23 --> 00:01:02.07
Instead, the tab asks the API, and the API downloads one archive of the repository

00:01:02.07 --> 00:01:05.27
at its head and lists its pull requests once.

00:01:05.27 --> 00:01:10.54
It parses the requirements, then matches each ID in the designs, journeys,

00:01:10.54 --> 00:01:13.18
test headers and pull request titles.

00:01:13.18 --> 00:01:15.67
That is recorded as A D R nineteen.

00:01:16.02 --> 00:01:18.08
The components are simpler too.

00:01:18.08 --> 00:01:23.59
One handler sits beside the artifacts handler, behind the same projects controller,

00:01:23.59 --> 00:01:26.65
and nothing needs indexing or keeping in step.

00:01:27.80 --> 00:01:32.97
One archive gives every file at once, and its top folder names the commit,

00:01:32.97 --> 00:01:36.54
so each answer says which short SHA it was read at.

00:01:36.54 --> 00:01:42.28
Before stage five there is no L2 file, so there are no requirements and no need to

00:01:42.28 --> 00:01:43.60
list pull requests.

00:01:43.95 --> 00:01:47.87
L2 headings must read L2, a number, a colon and a title.

00:01:47.87 --> 00:01:50.95
A heading that doesn't, or an ID used twice,

00:01:50.95 --> 00:01:56.48
is an error that names the commit and the line, rather than a half-built trace.

00:01:57.63 --> 00:02:02.15
Each L2 gets its L1, then everything that names its ID.

00:02:02.15 --> 00:02:08.89
An ID is matched whole, so L2 zero zero one is never found inside L2 zero zero one

00:02:08.89 --> 00:02:09.30
zero.

00:02:09.30 --> 00:02:15.55
No acceptance test is true only when no test names it and it isn't deferred.

00:02:15.90 --> 00:02:21.32
A test names a requirement only in its header: a traces to comment in the first

00:02:21.32 --> 00:02:24.68
twenty lines, in any of the usual comment styles.

00:02:24.68 --> 00:02:28.31
A later mention, or one in a document, doesn't count.

00:02:29.46 --> 00:02:34.04
In Mission Control, the list on the left searches by ID or title,

00:02:34.04 --> 00:02:36.29
and flags deferred requirements.

00:02:36.29 --> 00:02:40.59
The chosen L2 is in the address, so a link opens it directly.

00:02:40.59 --> 00:02:43.47
Its trace shows four columns with counts.

00:02:43.47 --> 00:02:49.11
Deferred shows its reason; otherwise a missing test shows the no acceptance test

00:02:49.11 --> 00:02:52.42
pill, with an icon and words, not colour alone.

00:02:53.57 --> 00:02:58.64
The backend tests came first, and failed with not found: there was no route.

00:02:58.64 --> 00:03:02.64
Six tests now cover the list and its L1s at the head commit,

00:03:02.64 --> 00:03:07.91
the four kinds of link with whole-ID matching, no acceptance test and deferred,

00:03:07.91 --> 00:03:13.51
a project before stage five, an unparsable heading that names its line, and Viewers.

00:03:13.86 --> 00:03:18.83
End to end, a live test runs a real worker through a whole direction,

00:03:18.83 --> 00:03:23.66
then opens L2 zero zero one and finds the design stage seven wrote,

00:03:23.66 --> 00:03:27.91
the journey from stage eight, the test stage ten committed,

00:03:27.91 --> 00:03:30.50
and the pull request that merged it.

00:03:30.85 --> 00:03:35.01
All green, the backend has two hundred and thirty eight tests,

00:03:35.01 --> 00:03:37.49
and the frontend four new view tests.

00:03:37.49 --> 00:03:43.13
End to end, there are eighteen new tests: five for behaviour, one live pipeline run,

00:03:43.13 --> 00:03:47.69
six visual comparisons with the mocks, and six accessibility checks.

00:03:48.84 --> 00:03:52.96
Here is Kiln Booking, built end to end by a real worker.

00:03:52.96 --> 00:03:57.01
Each of its requirements traces to a design, a journey,

00:03:57.01 --> 00:03:59.44
a test and a merged pull request.

00:03:59.79 --> 00:04:01.73
Ledgerline shows the gaps.

00:04:01.73 --> 00:04:07.11
L2 zero two zero has designs and a journey but no test, so it is marked.

00:04:07.11 --> 00:04:12.34
L2 zero three one is deferred, so its empty test column is not needed.

00:04:12.34 --> 00:04:17.35
And searching narrows the list, or says what range the project has.

00:04:17.70 --> 00:04:21.75
As a Viewer the trace reads the same in dark mode, and on a phone.

00:04:22.10 --> 00:04:25.33
Before stage five there are no requirements yet,

00:04:25.33 --> 00:04:29.64
while an L2 file that can't be parsed names the commit and line,

00:04:29.64 --> 00:04:32.13
and the repository is left unchanged.

00:04:33.28 --> 00:04:35.43
Journey seventeen is complete.

00:04:35.43 --> 00:04:40.44
Every L2 traces to its L1, designs, journeys, tests and pull requests,

00:04:40.44 --> 00:04:43.74
read straight from the repository at its head.

00:04:43.74 --> 00:04:48.11
A requirement with no test stands out, unless it is deferred.

00:04:48.11 --> 00:04:52.41
And the trace stays current as stages and increments finish.

00:04:52.41 --> 00:04:54.42
Next comes journey eighteen.
